I am testing recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text". The two reply texts are:

Reply A:
```json
{"a": "```"}
```
{"b": 2}

Reply B:
{"b": 2}

For Reply A, the fence holds the JSON object {"a": "```"}, so the rule requires the answer to be that object, i.e. {'a': '```'}. For Reply B, there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {'b': 2}. The function recover_json_object returns {'b': 2} for both replies, because the fence regex ```(?:json|JSON)?\s*(.*?)``` stops at the first ``` inside the string in Reply A, so the fenced candidate is truncated to `{"a": "` and is not a scoreable dict; the function then falls through to the top-level pool and scores the later {'b': 2}. This makes the two replies indistinguishable to the check even though the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if, on Reply A, recover_json_object actually returned {'a': '```'} (or raised), or if the documented rule were read as not requiring the fenced object to be scored when the fence contains a string with triple backticks.